specs/021-strata-inference-research/qwen-design.md:3:**Implementation status**: a reusable CPU GGUF decoder traverses all 48 blocks and preserves state across tokens; a two-token full-artifact smoke pass produces vocabulary logits. The MiniJinja tokenizer and Unix-socket worker are integrated. An optional CUDA matvec reaches only the final quantized vocabulary projection, and has not run on a GPU. Independent full-model parity, full rendered-prompt execution against the artifact, and CUDA execution for the remaining model operators are still open.
specs/021-strata-inference-research/qwen-design.md:8:- Tokenizer artifact: `tokenizer.json` from the same immutable checkpoint revision; pinned Nix hash `sha256-CZf0EMV6H05TsJ5L6PShctkO3ZVkNo+whHAwk3IpufM=`. It contains 248,077 addressable IDs (maximum ID 248,076), while the model embedding vocabulary is padded to 248,320. These are distinct dimensions and must not be conflated.
specs/021-strata-inference-research/qwen-design.md:9:- Chat template: `chat_template.jinja` from the same immutable checkpoint revision; pinned Nix hash `sha256-w8+eNKv0+eNsLXIWWqnBMtPipyW2wlhqqjqK+deoEEE=`. The exact template is retained as the semantic authority. MiniJinja 2.24 does not provide the template's `startswith`/`endswith` string methods by default, so the tokenizer registers a narrow unknown-method callback for those two string operations; the pinned template fixtures pass. Full-model tokenization and generation conformance remain unverified.
specs/021-strata-inference-research/qwen-design.md:11:`tokenizer::generate_greedy` provides the ordinary greedy control loop over a
specs/021-strata-inference-research/qwen-design.md:44:Checkpoint n-gram size is three with eight heads per n-gram length, producing sixteen 160-wide rows. Head vocabularies are successive primes at or above 20,000,000, with cumulative row offsets; the resulting logical table has 320,001,446 rows. Padding that row count to a multiple of 128 gives 320,001,536 physical rows. Each address is a per-head offset plus a remainder strictly below that head's prime vocabulary, so valid n-gram addresses stay within the logical extent and cannot reach the 90 physical padding rows. A test walks every model vocabulary token while evolving history and checks all 16 row IDs remain below the logical bound. A dense BF16/FP16 table at that padded size alone would occupy 102,400,491,520 bytes (about 95.4 GiB), before any other model weights. SplitMix64-derived odd multipliers use default seed 1234 and PLE-layer ordinal; products are bounded to signed 64-bit before XOR and per-head remainder. EOS is 248044; unavailable history is EOS-filled, and tokens after EOS may not see history from the preceding segment. The EOS token itself retains the preceding segment history.
specs/021-strata-inference-research/qwen-design.md:66:Create `agentix-qwen` for model-specific reference math, token/state handling, artifact identity and bounded storage; the generic inference crate retains lifecycle/selection ownership. Initial CPU math and storage do not link native bindings; the CPU crate uses pinned tokenizer/template and SHA-256 dependencies. This establishes independent deterministic oracles without committing to an unverified CUDA or quantized loader. No inference backend is registered until complete model execution and agentix contracts work.
